Epileptic Encephalopathies (Lennox-Gastaut Syndrome, Dravet Syndrome)
Acute Urgent Care
Multiple refractory seizure types, Progressive neurodevelopmental regression, Slow spike-and-wave on EEG, Drop attacks

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: AUC-030
Reason for Visit: Acute cluster of prolonged seizures and severe facial trauma following a violent "drop attack".
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient (child) and parents
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a pediatric patient with known Lennox-Gastaut Syndrome who suffered a sudden, violent atonic seizure resulting in a severe facial laceration, followed immediately by a cluster of prolonged tonic seizures. Urgent after-hours assessment was critical to administer rescue anti-epileptic medication, abort the escalating seizure cluster (preventing status epilepticus), and assess the traumatic head injury, as standard outpatient management is inadequate for this acute crisis.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: [Parent reports] He just suddenly lost all muscle tone, smashed his face on the table, and now he won't stop having these stiffening seizures.
History of Present Illness: The patient is a 6-year-old male with a known history of Lennox-Gastaut Syndrome, characterized by multiple refractory seizure types and progressive neurodevelopmental regression. Thirty minutes ago, the patient experienced a sudden atonic seizure (drop attack), resulting in a violent fall and blunt facial trauma. Since the fall, the patient has had three distinct, prolonged tonic seizures, each lasting 2-3 minutes, without fully regaining consciousness between episodes. The parents report pronounced behavioral abnormalities and severe hyperactivity prior to the event. The seizures are highly refractory to their standard, multiple anti-epileptic pharmacological therapies.
Functional Status: Currently post-ictal, lethargic, and bleeding from a facial laceration.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of progression to status epilepticus; significant carer burnout noted.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 110/70 | HR: 130 | Temp: 37.5 | RR: 24 | SpO2: 96% | Wt: 22 Kg
General: Patient is drowsy, post-ictal, with a 3cm actively oozing laceration over the left eyebrow.
Physical Exam: Neurological examination reveals a profoundly lethargic child who withdraws from painful stimuli but is not tracking visually. No focal neurological deficits are noted aside from the baseline profound intellectual disability. Muscular tone is flaccid post-seizure. Facial examination reveals a deep 3cm laceration over the left supraorbital ridge; pupils are equal and reactive to light, suggesting no massive intracranial hemorrhage, though a mild concussion is likely. Expected investigations in an outpatient setting would show an interictal awake EEG with classic slow spike-and-wave complexes (<2.5 Hz).
Environmental Observations: Parents have appropriately placed padding around the patient; helmet for drop attacks was not worn at the time of the incident.
Medication Reconciliation: Patient is on Valproate, Lamotrigine, and Clobazam. Missed morning dose of Clobazam noted.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Epileptic Encephalopathies (Lennox-Gastaut Syndrome, Dravet Syndrome) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Simple Febrile Seizure - Ruled out/unlikely due to the known history of severe epileptic encephalopathy, the presence of multiple seizure types (atonic followed by tonic), and the absence of a high fever.

Intracranial Haemorrhage (Traumatic) - Ruled out/unlikely as the primary cause of the ongoing seizures, as the drop attack preceded the trauma and the patient's pupils remain equal and reactive, though CT imaging is required to definitively rule this out.

Syncope/Arrhythmia - Ruled out/unlikely due to the witnessed tonic stiffening phases following the fall, which confirm epileptic activity rather than a simple cardiovascular faint.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient, with a known diagnosis of an epileptic encephalopathy characterized by multiple refractory seizure types and drop attacks, presents with an acute seizure cluster and secondary traumatic injury. The failure to regain baseline consciousness between seizures indicates impending status epilepticus. The urgent after-hours visit was absolutely essential to administer potent mucosal rescue medication to break the seizure cycle and to properly triage the head wound. Delaying intervention would likely result in irreversible neurological damage from prolonged status epilepticus and uncontrolled bleeding from the facial laceration.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered Midazolam 5mg via the buccal route immediately upon arrival as the patient began another tonic phase; seizure aborted within 2 minutes. Cleaned and applied Steri-Strips to the supraorbital laceration to achieve hemostasis. Advised immediate transfer to the pediatric emergency department for prolonged observation and consideration of a CT head.
Positive Behavioral Change: By ensuring your child always wears their protective soft helmet during all waking hours, you create a vital physical barrier that directly prevents these severe head injuries when a sudden drop attack occurs.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Ambulance transport to Pediatric ED.
DME/Safety (Optional): Reinforce strict use of protective headgear.
Follow-up: Pediatric Neurologist review required post-discharge to adjust baseline anticonvulsants.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Ambulance dispatched for safe transport. If another seizure begins en route and lasts longer than 5 minutes, or if the child stops breathing, the paramedics will intervene with intravenous medications and airway support.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The rescue medication has temporarily stopped the seizures, but because of the head injury and the cluster of attacks, he requires hospital monitoring.
Clinical Course: Children with this syndrome are prone to severe seizure clusters. The hospital team may need to adjust his baseline medications or administer an intravenous drip to prevent further attacks today.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Keep him lying on his side (recovery position) while he sleeps off the medication. This ensures his airway stays clear if he vomits. Do not attempt to give him any food, water, or oral medications right now.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: I gave him a strong muscle relaxant and anti-seizure medication directly into his cheek lining. It absorbs very quickly to stop the brain from misfiring. I have taped the cut, but a hospital doctor needs to review it to ensure no underlying bone damage.
Actionable Steps: Let the paramedics transport him so they can monitor his oxygen levels. Please pack all of his current medication bottles so the emergency doctors know exactly what doses he is on.

